"""
Railways Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: Indian Railways, History, Permanent Way (P-Way), Locomotives (WAP-7, WAG-12), LHB vs ICF, Vande Bharat, OHE (25 kV), Signalling, KAVACH, and DFC.
"""

rail_easy = [
    ("On which historic date did the first passenger commercial train run in India?", "16 April 1853", ["15 August 1947", "26 January 1950", "1 January 1900"], "The first commercial train ran between Bori Bunder (Bombay) and Thane over 34 kilometers with 14 carriages and 400 passengers."),
    ("The first passenger train in India ran between which two stations?", "Bombay (Bori Bunder) to Thane", ["Calcutta to Howrah", "Madras to Arkonam", "Delhi to Agra"], "The 34-km journey took 57 minutes, hauled by three steam locomotives named Sultan, Sindh, and Sahib."),
    ("What were the names of the three steam locomotives that hauled India's inaugural passenger train in 1853?", "Sultan, Sindh, and Sahib", ["Akbar, Birbal, and Tansen", "Ganga, Yamuna, and Saraswati", "Victoria, Albert, and George"], "Manufactured by Vulcan Foundry, the three steam locomotives hauled the Great Indian Peninsula Railway's historic train."),
    ("What is the track gauge width of 'Broad Gauge' (BG) used across Indian Railways?", "1,676 mm (5 feet 6 inches)", ["1,000 mm (1 meter)", "1,435 mm (Standard Gauge)", "762 mm (Narrow Gauge)"], "Indian Broad Gauge is 1,676 mm, one of the widest standard rail gauges globally, offering superior stability and volumetric capacity."),
    ("What is the track gauge width of 'Metre Gauge' (MG)?", "1,000 mm (1.0 meter)", ["1,676 mm", "1,435 mm", "762 mm"], "Metre gauge has a rail-to-rail distance of exactly 1 meter, largely converted to Broad Gauge under Project Unigauge."),
    ("What is the track gauge width of 'Standard Gauge' utilized primarily in Indian Metro rail networks?", "1,435 mm (4 feet 8.5 inches)", ["1,676 mm", "1,000 mm", "610 mm"], "Most modern urban metro transit systems (e.g., Delhi Metro, Bangalore Metro) use international Standard Gauge (1,435 mm)."),
    ("Where is the headquarters of the Railway Board and the Ministry of Railways located?", "Rail Bhavan, New Delhi", ["Churchgate, Mumbai", "Fairlie Place, Kolkata", "Park Town, Chennai"], "Rail Bhavan in New Delhi serves as the apex administrative headquarters of Indian Railways and the Railway Board."),
    ("In locomotive classification codes (e.g., WAP-7), what does the first letter 'W' indicate?", "Broad Gauge track (W = Wide / Broad Gauge)", ["Weight of the locomotive", "Western Railway zone", "West-facing driver cab"], "The first letter indicates track gauge: W = Broad Gauge (1676mm), Y = Metre Gauge, Z = Narrow Gauge (762mm)."),
    ("In locomotive codes (e.g., WAP-7 or WAG-9), what does the second letter 'A' indicate?", "AC Electric traction power supply", ["Automatic transmission", "Air-conditioned cabin", "Articulated wheel bogie"], "The second letter denotes motive power: A = AC electric (25 kV), D = Diesel, C = DC electric, CA = Dual AC/DC."),
    ("In locomotive codes (e.g., WAP-7 or WDG-4), what does the third letter 'P' indicate?", "Passenger traffic service", ["Petroleum tanker cargo", "Private commercial lease", "Primary shunting duty"], "The third letter indicates intended service: P = Passenger, G = Goods (freight), M = Mixed (passenger + goods), S = Shunting."),
    ("In locomotive codes (e.g., WAG-9 or WAG-12), what does the third letter 'G' indicate?", "Goods (Freight) traffic service", ["General passenger service", "Government executive special", "Gasoline-powered engine"], "'G' designates dedicated freight locomotives designed with high tractive effort and lower top speeds for heavy haulage."),
    ("Which Indian Railways electric locomotive boasts 12,000 horsepower (HP), manufactured in Madhepura, Bihar?", "WAG-12B", ["WAP-7", "WAG-9", "WDG-4"], "Manufactured as a joint venture with Alstom, the twin-section WAG-12B is one of the most powerful freight locomotives globally."),
    ("What standard AC electrical voltage is supplied through Overhead Equipment (OHE) for Indian Railways electrification?", "25 kV AC, single-phase, 50 Hz", ["11 kV DC", "1,500 V DC", "220 V AC, three-phase"], "Indian Railways utilizes 25 kV AC 50 Hz single-phase industrial frequency traction, stepping down through locomotive transformers."),
    ("What device mounted on the roof of an electric locomotive collects electrical current from the overhead wire?", "Pantograph", ["Trolley pole", "Third rail shoe", "Dynamo generator"], "The pantograph uses pneumatic pressure to maintain contact between its carbon collector strips and the energized contact wire."),
    ("What are the two modern main types of passenger coaches running on Indian Railways?", "LHB (Linke Hofmann Busch) and ICF (Integral Coach Factory)", ["Boeing and Airbus coaches", "Volvo and Scania coaches", "Metro and Monorail coaches"], "Conventional blue ICF coaches are being phased out in favor of safer, stainless-steel, anti-telescopic German LHB design coaches."),
    ("Where is the Integral Coach Factory (ICF) located in India?", "Perambur, Chennai (Tamil Nadu)", ["Kapurthala, Punjab", "Rae Bareli, Uttar Pradesh", "Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh"], "ICF Perambur is one of the world's largest rail coach manufacturers, pioneering the indigenous Vande Bharat Express."),
    ("Where is the Rail Coach Factory (RCF) located in India?", "Kapurthala, Punjab", ["Perambur, Chennai", "Chittaranjan, West Bengal", "Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh"], "RCF Kapurthala manufactures state-of-the-art LHB stainless steel coaches and specialized tourist stock."),
    ("Where is Chittaranjan Locomotive Works (CLW) located?", "Chittaranjan, Asansol (West Bengal)", ["Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh", "Madhepura, Bihar", "Patiala, Punjab"], "CLW is a premier electric locomotive manufacturing unit producing WAP-7 passenger and WAG-9 freight locomotives."),
    ("Where is Banaras Locomotive Works (BLW, formerly DLW) located?", "Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh", ["Chittaranjan, West Bengal", "Jhansi, Uttar Pradesh", "Gwalior, Madhya Pradesh"], "BLW manufactures modern high-horsepower diesel and electric locomotives for Indian Railways and global exports."),
    ("What is the popular commercial name of 'Train 18', India's first indigenous semi-high-speed trainset?", "Vande Bharat Express", ["Gatimaan Express", "Tejas Express", "Duronto Express"], "Designed and built at ICF Chennai, Vande Bharat Express operates on distributed power EMU technology with 160 km/h capability."),
    ("What distinguishes 'Vande Bharat Express' from traditional locomotive-hauled passenger trains?", "Distributed Power (Trainset) architecture with motorized bogies under alternate passenger coaches (no standalone locomotive)", ["It runs on rubber tires on asphalt roads", "It is powered by a steam boiler engine", "It is an unpowered carriage pulled by tractors"], "Distributed traction provides rapid acceleration and deceleration, cutting transit times by up to 15-20%."),
    ("What type of automatic coupler is used on modern LHB coaches to prevent coaches from climbing over each other in collisions?", "CBC (Centre Buffer Coupler) with Anti-Climbing feature", ["Traditional Screw Coupling with side buffers", "Hook and Link coupler", "Pin and Link coupler"], "Tightlock AAR CBC couplers absorb collision energy and prevent coaches from capsizing or telescoping into one another."),
    ("What color are the signal aspects in Indian Railways Multi-Aspect Colour Light Signalling (MACLS)?", "Green, Double Yellow, Yellow, and Red", ["Green, Blue, Orange, and Red", "White, Yellow, Green, and Black", "Red, Pink, Yellow, and White"], "MACLS aspects convey clear speed commands: Green (Proceed), Double Yellow (Attention), Yellow (Caution), and Red (Danger/Stop)."),
    ("In railway signaling, what does a 'Red' aspect displayed on a fixed stop signal instruct the train driver?", "Danger: Stop dead before the signal", ["Proceed at maximum authorized speed", "Proceed with caution at 30 km/h", "Accelerate to clear the track block"], "A red aspect represents an absolute prohibition; passing a red signal without authority is the severe safety offense of SPAD."),
    ("In railway safety, what does the acronym 'SPAD' stand for?", "Signal Passed at Danger", ["Speed Protection And Deceleration", "Station Platform Arrival Delay", "Safety Protocol for Automated Departure"], "SPAD occurs when an unauthorized train passes a red stop signal, posing catastrophic risks of head-on or rear-end collisions."),
    ("What is 'KAVACH' developed by Research Designs and Standards Organisation (RDSO)?", "India's indigenous Automatic Train Protection (ATP) system that prevents collisions and automatically applies brakes", ["A bulletproof vest worn by railway ticket inspectors", "A metal fence built along station platform tracks", "A mobile application for ordering food on trains"], "KAVACH (SIL-4 certified) continuously monitors train speed, alerts drivers in cab, and brakes automatically to prevent SPAD."),
    ("What is the primary role of RDSO in Indian Railways?", "Research Designs and Standards Organisation (the technical advisor and R&D institution of the Ministry of Railways in Lucknow)", ["Auditing annual passenger ticket reservation revenue", "Managing catering services in railway dining cars", "Conducting police criminal trials for train thefts"], "RDSO sets technical specifications, tests prototypes, and issues engineering codes for track, locomotives, and signaling."),
    ("What independent statutory authority investigates serious train accidents in India, working under the Ministry of Civil Aviation?", "Commission of Railway Safety (CRS / CCRS)", ["Railway Board Safety Cell", "National Investigation Agency (NIA)", "Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI)"], "Working under Civil Aviation (not Railways) ensures complete administrative independence and objectivity during accident inquiries."),
    ("What are the concrete cross-ties that support the steel rails in modern railway track called?", "Pre-Stressed Concrete (PSC) Sleepers", ["Wooden timber ties", "Cast iron pot sleepers", "Steel trough sleepers"], "PSC sleepers provide high track modulus, durability (50+ years), electrical insulation for track circuits, and lateral track stability."),
    ("What is the crushed stone layer placed under and around railway track sleepers called?", "Ballast", ["Asphalt", "Subgrade soil", "Grout cement"], "Ballast distributes heavy axle loads, provides track drainage, and offers lateral and longitudinal resistance against track buckling."),
    ("What device fixes and clamps the steel rail firmly onto the concrete sleeper?", "Elastic Rail Clip (ERC / Pandrol Clip)", ["Standard steel construction nails", "Rubber suction cups", "Plastic adhesive tape"], "Spring-steel ERC clips provide consistent toe load (~850-1100 kg), holding rails securely against thermal expansion and dynamic vibration."),
    ("What is a 'Level Crossing' (LC Gate) in railway operations?", "An intersection where a railway track crosses a road or highway at the same physical elevation", ["A junction where two railway tracks cross each other", "A pedestrian foot overbridge built across tracks", "A maintenance inspection pit in train depots"], "LC gates feature mechanical or electrical lifting barriers; Indian Railways is actively eliminating manned/unmanned LC gates via ROBs/RUBs."),
    ("What does 'ROB' and 'RUB' stand for in railway infrastructure upgrade programmes?", "Road Over Bridge (flyover) and Road Under Bridge (underpass)", ["Railway Operational Base and Railway Urban Buffer", "Rolling-stock Overhaul Bay and Rail Utility Block", "Route Optimization Board and Rapid Unloading Berth"], "Constructing ROBs and RUBs eliminates surface level crossings, eradicating collision risks and accelerating highway and train traffic."),
    ("What does 'IRCTC' stand for in Indian Railways public service delivery?", "Indian Railway Catering and Tourism Corporation", ["Indian Rail Construction and Transport Council", "International Rail Cargo Transit Corporation", "Integrated Rail Communication and Tracking Center"], "IRCTC operates the national internet passenger ticketing portal, onboard train catering, packaged drinking water (Rail Neer), and tourism.")
]

rail_med = [
    ("In railway track engineering, what is the weight designation of the standard heavy rail section used on major broad gauge routes?", "60 kg per meter rail (60 kg/m or UIC 60)", ["30 kg per meter", "90 kg per meter", "120 kg per meter"], "60 kg/m rails (with 90 UTS tensile strength) support heavy 25-ton axle loads, high speeds up to 160 km/h, and high gross million tons (GMT)."),
    ("In track engineering, what does '90 UTS' designate for rail metallurgy?", "Ultimate Tensile Strength of at least 90 kg per square millimeter (approx. 880 MPa)", ["Rail manufactured in the year 1990", "A maximum train speed limit of 90 km/h", "A rail weight of 90 pounds per yard"], "90 UTS rails provide superior wear resistance and fatigue life against rolling contact fatigue (RCF) and head checks."),
    ("What is 'LWR' and 'CWR' in modern railway track welding technology?", "Long Welded Rail (rails welded into lengths > 250 m) and Continuous Welded Rail (welded continuously through stations without fishplates)", ["Light Weight Rail and Cast Welded Rail", "Line Wire Relay and Circuit Wire Receiver", "Lateral Wheel Resistance and Central Wheel Rail"], "LWR/CWR eliminates noisy, high-wear fishplated rail joints, providing a smooth seamless ride and slashing track maintenance costs."),
    ("What welding process is universally used in the field to join rail ends during track renewal?", "Alumino-Thermic (AT) Welding (Thermite Welding) and Flash Butt Welding", ["Oxy-acetylene gas torch welding", "Submerged arc flux welding", "Cold plastic chemical soldering"], "Thermite reaction between iron oxide and aluminum powder produces molten steel at 2,500°C, casting rail joints in situ."),
    ("In track engineering, what is 'Track Buckling' and what environmental condition triggers it?", "Sudden lateral distortion of track caused by excessive compressive thermal expansion forces during extreme summer heat", ["Cracking of rails caused by freezing winter ice", "Sinking of sleepers into soft mud during monsoon floods", "Erosion of ballast by high-velocity windstorms"], "If the actual rail temperature exceeds the destressing temperature (t_d) without adequate ballast resistance, rails buckle violently."),
    ("In railway engineering, what is 'Cant' or 'Superelevation' on curved tracks?", "Raising the outer rail higher than the inner rail on a curve to counteract centrifugal force pushing the train outward", ["Lowering both rails into an underground trench", "Widening the track gauge to allow larger wheels", "Smoothing the rail head with abrasive grinding trains"], "Cant tilts the train inward, balancing centrifugal acceleration so passengers experience comfort and wheels avoid flange climbing."),
    ("In track dynamics, what is 'Cant Deficiency'?", "The difference between the theoretical equilibrium cant required for higher speed and the actual cant provided on track", ["A structural failure where the outer rail collapses", "A shortage of crushed stone ballast on curves", "An error made by civil surveyor engineers"], "Cant deficiency allows fast passenger trains to run safely on curves without tilting freight trains excessively on the same track."),
    ("In railway braking systems, what is the standard operating air pressure maintained in the Brake Pipe (BP)?", "5.0 kg per square centimeter (approx. 5 bar or 71 psi)", ["10.0 kg per square centimeter", "1.0 kg per square centimeter", "25.0 kg per square centimeter"], "Charging the BP to 5.0 kg/cm2 releases train brakes; exhausting pressure causes the distributor valve to apply brakes automatically."),
    ("In twin-pipe air brake systems on passenger trains, what is the operating pressure of the Feed Pipe (FP)?", "6.0 kg per square centimeter (approx. 6 bar)", ["5.0 kg per square centimeter", "2.0 kg per square centimeter", "12.0 kg per square centimeter"], "The Feed Pipe (6 kg/cm2) continuously replenishes auxiliary air reservoirs on coaches, enabling rapid brake release and re-application."),
    ("In railway brake mechanics, why are train air brakes designed as 'Fail-Safe'?", "Any accidental rupture or decoupling of the air brake hose vents pressure to atmosphere, causing brakes to apply automatically", ["Brakes require continuous electric power to remain locked", "Brakes must be manually pumped by passengers during stops", "Brakes only operate when the train is moving downhill"], "A severed brake pipe automatically dumps air pressure, engaging emergency braking across every coach without driver intervention."),
    ("What is the primary role of the 'Distributor Valve' (DV) on an air-braked railway coach?", "Sensing pressure drops in the Brake Pipe and proportionally directing compressed air from the auxiliary reservoir into brake cylinders", ["Distributing electricity to passenger air conditioning fans", "Balancing water supply across coach toilet tanks", "Controlling the speed of the locomotive diesel engine"], "The DV is the heart of the air brake system, executing gradual application, emergency application, and gradual release."),
    ("In railway braking technology, what is 'Regenerative Braking' on 3-phase electric locomotives (WAP-7, WAG-9)?", "Converting kinetic momentum into electrical power via traction motors acting as generators, feeding clean electricity back into the OHE grid", ["Heating friction brake shoes until they wear out completely", "Spraying compressed air against the rotating wheel flanges", "Deploying aerodynamic parachutes from the back of the train"], "Regenerative braking saves up to 20-30% of traction energy, reduces mechanical brake shoe wear, and generates zero pollution."),
    ("In electric traction OHE architecture, what is a 'Neutral Section' (Phase Break)?", "A short insulated dead zone in the overhead wire separating electrical feeds from two different sub-stations to prevent phase short-circuits", ["A section of track where passenger trains are banned", "A railway station located on international border boundaries", "A waiting room built for railway security staff"], "Electric locomotives coast through neutral sections with the main circuit breaker (DJ/VCB) open to prevent 25 kV inter-phase arcing."),
    ("What are the two structural wires that make up an Overhead Equipment (OHE) catenary system?", "The upper 'Catenary Wire' (supporting wire) and the lower 'Contact Wire' (grooved copper wire touched by the pantograph)", ["The Live Wire and the Earth Neutral Wire", "The Positive Copper Wire and Negative Iron Wire", "The High Voltage Wire and Low Voltage Wire"], "Droppers suspend the horizontal contact wire from the sagging parabolic catenary wire at uniform height above the rails."),
    ("In modern railway signaling, what is 'Electronic Interlocking' (EI)?", "A microprocessor-based digital safety system controlling points and signals based on Boolean logic to prevent conflicting train routes", ["A mechanical system using physical iron levers and wire pulleys", "A system where signals are locked using brass padlocks and keys", "An automatic ticketing gate installed at station entrances"], "EI replaces bulky electro-mechanical relays, providing SIL-4 fail-safe hardware redundancy and flexible software route setting."),
    ("What is an 'Axle Counter' and why is it preferred over traditional DC track circuits?", "Detects wheel axles entering/exiting a section; immune to flood submergence, poor ballast drainage, and rusted rail surfaces", ["A device that measures passenger body weight on coaches", "A tool used to lubricate locomotive wheel bearings", "An optical camera photographing train car side numbers"], "Traditional track circuits fail when tracks flood or ballast is fouled; electronic axle counters operate reliably in all weather."),
    ("What does 'MSDAC' stand for in advanced railway signaling?", "Multi-Section Digital Axle Counter", ["Main Station Direct Access Controller", "Modern Signalling Data Acquisition Circuit", "Multiple Switch Delivery Action Command"], "MSDAC monitors multiple contiguous track blocks across large station yards, feeding track vacancy data directly to Electronic Interlocking."),
    ("In railway signaling, what is the 'Block Section'?", "The section of running track between two block stations into which no train may enter until Line Clear has been granted", ["A concrete barrier placed across the tracks to halt trains", "The passenger waiting platform located inside stations", "A specialized repair bay inside locomotive maintenance sheds"], "Block working enforces space-interval separation, guaranteeing that two trains are never permitted inside the same block section simultaneously."),
    ("In railway signaling, what is the primary operational difference between 'Absolute Block' and 'Automatic Block' systems?", "Absolute Block allows only ONE train between two adjacent stations; Automatic Block uses signals to permit multiple trains running in sequence", ["Absolute Block uses computers; Automatic Block uses paper tokens", "Absolute Block is for metro trains; Automatic Block for freight", "There is zero difference; both systems are identical"], "Automatic block signaling divides track into shorter 1-2 km signal blocks, drastically multiplying route capacity."),
    ("What is 'Headway' in railway transit planning?", "The time interval between two successive trains traveling on the same track in the same direction", ["The height clearance under overhead railway bridges", "The diameter of the locomotive front headlight lamp", "The passenger seating capacity of the front executive coach"], "Shorter headway (achieved through automatic signaling and high acceleration) increases line throughput and passenger train frequency."),
    ("What is 'Dedicated Freight Corridor' (DFC) in Indian Railways infrastructure?", "High-capacity, high-speed rail corridors built exclusively for freight trains with 32.5-ton axle load capability and double-stack container clearance", ["A single track reserved exclusively for executive luxury tourist trains", "A dedicated underground tunnel system for transporting military tanks", "An elevated monorail network carrying express postal mail packages"], "DFCs (Western DFC and Eastern DFC) segregate heavy freight from passenger traffic, slashing logistics costs and transit times."),
    ("What is the unique technological clearance feature of the Western Dedicated Freight Corridor (WDFC)?", "High-rise OHE (7.1 meters contact wire height) permitting double-stack container freight trains hauled by electric locomotives", ["Underground electrified third-rail power supply", "Tracks constructed without any crushed ballast stones", "Trains running on wooden timber track rails"], "WDFC is the first electrified railway globally to operate double-stack shipping containers under high-rise overhead catenary."),
    ("What is 'RO-RO' (Roll-On Roll-Off) service pioneered by Konkan Railway?", "Transporting loaded commercial road trucks directly on open railway flat wagons (BRN/BOM) across congested or difficult terrain", ["A passenger train where travelers roll their own luggage", "An amusement park roller-coaster built near rail stations", "A circular train route that operates without any stops"], "RO-RO saves truck diesel fuel, cuts highway carbon emissions, prevents highway accidents, and accelerates cargo delivery."),
    ("In railway track monitoring, what does 'WILD' stand for?", "Wheel Impact Load Detector (trackside sensors measuring dynamic impact forces from defective, flat, or out-of-round wheels)", ["Wireless Internet Line Delivery", "Wagon Inventory and Logistics Database", "Wide-angle Infrastructure Laser Detector"], "WILD detects flat wheel spots before severe hammering impact fractures steel rails or shatters roller bearings."),
    ("In railway safety, what is a 'Hot Box Detector' (HBD)?", "Infrared trackside sensors scanning passing axle bearings to detect dangerous abnormal overheating before catastrophic bearing seizure", ["A heated insulated food delivery box used in passenger pantry cars", "A temperature sensor measuring the heat of passenger diesel engines", "A fire alarm system installed inside station baggage rooms"], "Overheated axle bearings can cause axle burn-off and violent derailments; HBD alerts station controllers to stop the train immediately."),
    ("What is the 'Amrit Bharat Station Scheme' launched by the Ministry of Railways?", "A nationwide master-planning initiative modernizing and transforming over 1,300 railway stations into modern city centers with world-class amenities", ["A scheme distributing free drinking water cups on train platforms", "A project replacing all train tracks with magnetic levitation rails", "A policy banning all private commercial shops inside rail stations"], "Amrit Bharat stations feature roof plazas, intermodal integration, aesthetic local architecture, wide concourses, and green energy."),
    ("What is 'Fog PASS' device in train winter operations?", "A GPS-based handheld device placed in the locomotive cab that provides audio-visual warnings to drivers approaching upcoming signals in dense fog", ["A chemical smoke grenade thrown from trains to alert stations", "A special passenger ticket sold during foggy winter months", "A high-powered heat fan blowing warm air onto tracks"], "Fog PASS displays distance to upcoming signals, level crossing gates, and speed restrictions, enabling safe operations during zero-visibility winter fogs."),
    ("In railway passenger coach bogie design, what is 'FIAT Bogie' used on LHB coaches?", "A modern cast-steel bogie featuring primary coil springs, secondary air bellows, hydraulic dampers, and disc brakes rated for 160-200 km/h", ["A wooden frame bogie used on colonial vintage steam heritage trains", "A bogie that runs on rubber tires on elevated concrete tracks", "A single-axle truck bogie designed exclusively for freight wagons"], "FIAT bogies provide superior high-speed ride index, excellent lateral stability, and pneumatic secondary air suspension for passenger comfort."),
    ("What is 'Air Spring' (Air Suspension Bellow) in modern LHB coaches and EMUs?", "Pressurized rubber air bellows replacing metal secondary coil springs, maintaining constant coach floor height regardless of passenger load", ["An inflatable rubber cushion placed on passenger sleeping berths", "A pneumatic air blower circulating cool air inside air-conditioned compartments", "An emergency airbag deploying from the front of the locomotive during collisions"], "Air springs automatically inflate or deflate based on passenger passenger weight, optimizing ride comfort and protecting bogie components."),
    ("In track maintenance engineering, what is a 'Tamping Machine' (Track Tamper)?", "A self-propelled track maintenance machine that lifts the track, corrects alignment and cross-levels, and packs ballast under sleepers", ["A machine that paints steel rails with protective rust coatings", "A vehicle that cleans passenger coach toilet tanks at depots", "A crane that lifts derailed train coaches back onto the rails"], "Mechanized tamping (e.g., Plasser & Theurer) restores geometric track parameters (alignment, top, twist, gauge) at high speed."),
    ("In railway freight logistics, what is 'FOIS'?", "Freight Operations Information System (a centralized digital logistics management system tracking all freight wagons, rakes, and consignments in real time)", ["Fleet Ownership and Inspection Standard", "Fuel Optimization and Ignition System", "Federal Overseas Import Schedule"], "FOIS manages freight scheduling, electronic railway receipts (e-RR), rake allocation, and real-time transit tracking across India."),
    ("What is 'COIS' (or ICMS) in railway passenger management?", "Coaching Operations Information System (monitoring real-time movement, punctuality, and maintenance status of all passenger coaches and rakes)", ["Centralized Office Internet Service", "Customer Order Identification Standard", "Commercial Operations Insurance Scheme"], "ICMS tracks coach utilization, predicts rake maintenance overhauls (POH/IOH), and monitors passenger train punctuality across zones."),
    ("What does 'MEMU' stand for in Indian Railways commuter passenger services?", "Mainline Electric Multiple Unit", ["Metro Electrical Maintenance Unit", "Major Express Mobile Unit", "Municipal Electric Mobility Utility"], "MEMUs operate on 25 kV AC overhead catenary lines, providing frequent, high-acceleration semi-urban and inter-city commuter connectivity.")
]

rail_hard = [
    ("In railway track structural design, what is the 'Talbot Formula' (or Zimmermann Method) used to calculate?", "Bending moments and rail deflection under dynamic wheel loads by treating the rail as a continuously supported elastic foundation", ["The maximum passenger ticket revenue generated per kilometer", "The stopping distance of trains running on steep downhill gradients", "The acoustic decibel noise produced by diesel locomotive exhausts"], "A.N. Talbot established track modulus equations (u = load per unit length per unit deflection) governing dynamic rail bending stresses."),
    ("In railway vehicle-track dynamics, what does 'Nadal's Formula' determine for wheel climb derailment safety?", "The critical derailment limit ratio of lateral force to vertical wheel load: (L/V) = [tan(delta) - mu] / [1 + mu * tan(delta)]", ["The maximum tractive effort of electric locomotive motors", "The fuel consumption of diesel engines per hour of operation", "The braking distance of emergency air-braked freight rakes"], "Nadal's ratio dictates when a wheel flange will climb the rail head; if L/V exceeds ~0.8 to 1.2 (depending on wheel flange angle delta and friction mu), derailment occurs."),
    ("In track maintenance engineering, what is 'Track Structure Modulus' (u)?", "The supporting stiffness of the permanent way, defined as the load per unit length of rail required to depress the track by one unit of deflection", ["The total financial cost of laying one kilometer of broad gauge track", "The percentage of iron ore present in steel rail alloy metallurgy", "The electrical resistance of the concrete sleeper insulation pad"], "Track modulus (typically 200 to 350 kg/cm/cm for PSC track) determines track elasticity, dynamic wave velocity, and ride quality."),
    ("In railway signaling engineering, what is the 'Fail-Safe Principle: Closed Circuit Principle'?", "Circuits are designed so that any physical break, power loss, or component failure de-energizes the relay, causing the signal to drop to RED (Danger)", ["Circuits are wired in an unbroken circle around the station building", "Signals are kept permanently green unless manual switches are turned", "Circuits operate without any electrical batteries or wires"], "Gravity drops signal relays to de-energized positions; any open-circuit fault, broken wire, or short-circuit defaults safely to Danger."),
    ("In track engineering, what is 'Destressing of LWR' (Long Welded Rail)?", "The operation of adjusting internal rail thermal stresses to zero at a designated Mean Rail Temperature (t_o or t_d) before final fastening", ["Painting the rail head with white cooling thermal paint in summer", "Relaxing locomotive drivers between long passenger driving shifts", "Replacing crushed ballast stones with fresh quarry river gravel"], "Destressing prevents rail breaks in winter (tensile stress) and track buckling in summer (compressive stress) by locking in neutral stress."),
    ("What is 'Rail Neutral Temperature' (Stress-Free Temperature, t_o)?", "The rail temperature at which the steel rail is completely free from internal longitudinal thermal tension or compression", ["The freezing point of water on outdoor railway tracks (0°C)", "The maximum summer temperature recorded on Indian plains (50°C)", "The boiling temperature of lubricating locomotive engine oil"], "Destressing sets t_o within a safe zone (typically t_m + 5°C to t_m + 10°C, where t_m is mean annual rail temperature), balancing summer and winter forces."),
    ("In railway track geometry, what is 'Track Twist' and why is it a severe derailment hazard?", "The algebraic difference in cross-levels over a specific base length (e.g., 3.6 m), which can cause wheel unloading on diagonal wheels", ["A spiral deformation of the steel rail caused by extreme heat", "The physical rotation of concrete sleepers inside ballast beds", "The turning of locomotive axles during sharp junction curves"], "Severe twist lifts one wheel off the rail head; with reduced vertical wheel load (V), Nadal's L/V threshold is breached, triggering wheel climb."),
    ("In locomotive propulsion engineering, what is the role of the 'Traction Inverter' (Variable Voltage Variable Frequency - VVVF) in WAP-7 / WAG-9?", "Converting 25 kV stepped-down/rectified DC link power into variable voltage, variable frequency 3-phase AC to control induction motors smoothly", ["Converting diesel fuel into high-pressure compressed air", "Charging the 110V auxiliary battery of passenger coaches", "Cooling the locomotive transformer oil through radiator fans"], "VVVF inverters using IGBTs deliver stepless acceleration, high starting tractive effort, slip-slide control, and efficient regenerative braking."),
    ("In electric locomotives, what are 'IGBTs' (Insulated Gate Bipolar Transistors) compared to older GTO thyristors?", "Modern power semiconductor switches offering higher switching frequencies, lower power losses, silent operation, and compact water-cooled converters", ["Mechanical copper contact switches operated by air pistons", "Heavy iron flywheels storing rotational kinetic energy", "Transformer coils wound with high-purity aluminum wire"], "IGBT-based traction converters revolutionized locomotive efficiency, replacing noisy GTO thyristors with microsecond solid-state switching."),
    ("In railway rolling stock safety, what is 'Anti-Climbing Mechanism' integrated into AAR Tightlock couplers?", "Interlocking vertical interlocking lugs on the coupler faces that physically prevent adjacent coaches from riding up and overriding each other during collision", ["A sharp metal spike preventing passengers from climbing on train roofs", "An alarm system warning passengers not to board moving trains", "A rubber bumper absorbing low-speed station coupling shocks"], "Anti-climbing locks prevent vertical telescoping, keeping coaches upright in linear derailment zones and shielding passenger cabins."),
    ("In railway braking systems, what is the 'Emergency Application' discharge rate of the Brake Pipe?", "Venting BP pressure from 5.0 kg/cm2 to zero in less than 3 seconds through large-bore driver emergency brake valves", ["Slowly dropping BP pressure over a period of 15 minutes", "Increasing BP pressure from 5.0 kg/cm2 to 10.0 kg/cm2", "Reversing the flow of air from feed pipe into brake pipe"], "Rapid venting triggers the highest emergency brake cylinder pressure (3.8 kg/cm2 on LHB), achieving minimum emergency stopping distance (ESD)."),
    ("In train dynamics, what is 'Emergency Stopping Distance' (ESD) for 130 km/h passenger trains on Indian Railways broad gauge?", "Typically under 1,000 to 1,200 meters under full emergency service air braking on level tangent track", ["Under 50 meters", "Over 5,000 meters (5 kilometers)", "Exactly 200 meters"], "Signaling block lengths and warning distances are calculated based on verified ESD curves to guarantee trains can halt before stop signals."),
    ("In railway signaling, what is 'Approach Locking' on interlocking route panels?", "A safety lock preventing an operator from changing points or revoking a cleared route once an approaching train has entered the approach track circuit", ["Locking the station master's office door when trains enter the yard", "A mechanical lock preventing passengers from approaching tracks", "An automated turnstile gate locking passenger platform access"], "Approach locking prevents an operator from taking away a signal in front of a train that can no longer stop before the signal."),
    ("What is 'Route Release Locking' (Sectional Route Release) in modern railway interlocking?", "Sub-routes within a complex junction yard release sequentially behind the train as each track section is cleared, freeing points for other routes", ["Unlocking all station signals simultaneously at midnight", "Allowing train drivers to choose their own track routes freely", "Releasing emergency brakes automatically on passenger coaches"], "Sectional release maximizes yard throughput: trailing points can be aligned for subsequent train movements without waiting for the entire train to exit the yard."),
    ("In railway telecommunications, what is 'GSM-R' and 'LTE-R'?", "Dedicated digital wireless cellular standards for railway mobile voice communication, cab signaling, and Automatic Train Protection data telemetry", ["A commercial satellite television broadcast network for train passengers", "An unencrypted citizen band radio frequency used by truck drivers", "A fiber optic cable laid inside railway track steel rails"], "GSM-R (and upcoming 5G/LTE-R) guarantees quality-of-service, functional addressing, emergency group calls, and secure data for train control systems."),
    ("In electrical traction OHE engineering, what is 'Stagger' of the contact wire?", "Deliberately zig-zagging the contact wire laterally (+/- 200 mm) across the track center line on successive support masts", ["Varying the vertical height of the wire randomly above rails", "Using two different types of copper wire along the track", "Leaving the wire loose so it sways freely in windstorms"], "Stagger prevents the localized contact wire from grooving a single deep trench into the pantograph carbon strip, ensuring uniform strip wear."),
    ("What is 'Contact Wire Gradient' in OHE design?", "The permissible rate of change of contact wire height per unit distance (e.g., 2 mm/m) when approaching bridges or tunnels", ["The electrical voltage drop measured across ten kilometers of wire", "The temperature rise of the wire under heavy traction current", "The physical slope of the track gradient on mountain passes"], "Permissible gradients ensure the high-speed pantograph can glide smoothly upward or downward without losing physical electrical contact."),
    ("In high-speed traction, what is 'Pantograph Bounce' and why is it dangerous?", "Momentary physical separation of the pantograph carbon strip from the contact wire, causing severe electrical arcing and wire erosion", ["The vertical vibration of passenger coach seat cushions on curves", "The bouncing of train wheels on rough rail joints", "A mechanical jumping mechanism used to clear level crossings"], "Arcing melts contact wire copper, damages carbon collector strips, causes electromagnetic radio interference, and risks catenary wire snappage."),
    ("In track structural health monitoring, what does a 'Track Recording Car' (TRC) measure?", "Continuous laser and chord measurements of track geometry parameters: Gauge, Alignment, Unevenness, Twist, and Cross-level", ["The acoustic sound volume of passing passenger train horns", "The total weight of passenger luggage carried on coaches", "The number of birds nesting on overhead catenary masts"], "TRC runs under load, generating Track Quality Index (TQI) scores to prioritize mechanized tamping and track maintenance."),
    ("What does 'TQI' (Track Quality Index) evaluate in Indian Railways permanent way maintenance?", "A composite standard deviation index quantifying track geometric irregularities over kilometer blocks (lower TQI = superior track quality)", ["The percentage of passenger seats occupied on express trains", "The speed at which track maintenance laborers dig ballast", "The total financial cost of laying new pre-stressed concrete sleepers"], "TQI mathematically combines standard deviations of unevenness, twist, gauge, and alignment; low TQI certifies track for 130-160 km/h operation."),
    ("In railway civil engineering, what is the 'Ruling Gradient' of a section?", "The steepest maximum gradient permitted on a railway section that dictates the maximum train load that can be hauled by a locomotive", ["The average slope of river water flowing under railway bridges", "The cross-level slope of passenger station platform floors", "The maximum gradient allowed on pedestrian footpaths"], "Ruling gradient (e.g., 1 in 100 or 1 in 200) determines whether banking locomotives (pusher engines) are required on mountain ghat sections."),
    ("In railway track alignment, what is a 'Transition Curve' (Clothoid / Cubic Parabola)?", "A curve of constantly changing curvature inserted between straight track and circular curve to introduce cant and curvature gradually", ["A sharp 90-degree right-angle turn in railway station tracks", "A vertical hill crest where track climbs over highway flyovers", "A temporary diversion track built during bridge reconstruction"], "Transition curves prevent sudden jolts, linearly ramping up lateral centrifugal acceleration and superelevation smoothly."),
    ("In rolling stock maintenance, what is 'POH' and 'IOH' in railway workshop schedules?", "Periodic Overhaul (complete strip-down and overhaul in centralized workshops) and Intermediate Overhaul (conducted in maintenance depots)", ["Passenger Order Handling and Internal Operations Header", "Primary Operating Hours and Intermediate Operating Hours", "Project Overhaul Plan and Integrated Operations Hub"], "POH (every 18 months to 6 years depending on stock) completely dismantles bogies, bearings, and brake gear for non-destructive testing."),
    ("In non-destructive testing (NDT) of railway axles and rails, what is 'Ultrasonic Flaw Detection' (USFD)?", "Using high-frequency ultrasonic sound waves to detect internal microscopic fatigue cracks, head checks, and flaws inside steel rails and axles", ["X-raying complete train coaches using medical radiation hospital machines", "Testing rail hardness by striking rails with heavy steel sledgehammers", "Listening to train noise with acoustic stethoscope headphones"], "USFD testing detects transverse fatigue cracks before rails fracture under dynamic wheel loads, preventing rail-fracture derailments."),
    ("In wheel dynamics, what is a 'Flat Tyre' (Wheel Flat) and what causes it?", "A localized flat spot worn onto the circular wheel tread circumference caused by wheel skidding during severe braking with locked brakes", ["A puncture in a rubber tire on maintenance road vehicles", "A cracked glass window pane in passenger coach doors", "A bent steel rail that causes trains to lean sideways"], "Skidding grinds a flat chord into the wheel; rolling flat spots generate hammer-like impact loads that fracture rails and wreck bearings."),
    ("In locomotive diesel engineering, what is a 'Turbocharger' and how does it function?", "A turbine driven by exhaust gases that forces compressed air into engine cylinders, boosting combustion efficiency and horsepower output", ["An electric motor that rotates locomotive cooling water radiator fans", "A mechanical gear that switches transmission between forward and reverse", "A device that injects diesel fuel directly into passenger train exhaust"], "Turbocharging enables compact locomotive diesel engines (e.g., 16-cylinder EMD) to generate up to 4,500 horsepower efficiently at high altitudes."),
    ("In railway operating economics, what does 'Operating Ratio' (OR) evaluate?", "Working expenses expressed as a percentage of gross traffic earnings: OR = (Total Working Expenses / Gross Traffic Receipts) * 100", ["The percentage of passenger seats booked on express trains", "The ratio of diesel locomotives to electric locomotives", "The average running speed of passenger trains across zones"], "An Operating Ratio of 95% means Indian Railways spends 95 paise to earn one rupee; a lower OR indicates healthy financial surpluses for capital investment."),
    ("In freight logistics, what is 'Merry-Go-Round' (MGR) railway system?", "A dedicated closed-loop dedicated rail transit system linking coal mines directly to thermal power plant unloading hoppers with bottom-discharge wagons", ["A heritage steam passenger train operating around holiday resorts", "A turntable used to rotate locomotives inside maintenance sheds", "A container freight train connecting sea ports to dry ports"], "MGR trains operate on continuous rapid loading/unloading cycles without uncoupling locomotives, transporting millions of tons of coal efficiently."),
    ("What is 'KAVACH' technological communication mechanism for continuous train location determination?", "Radio Frequency Identification (RFID) tags installed along track center lines at 1 km intervals and high-frequency UHF radio data modems", ["Standard public cellular 4G mobile SIM cards only", "Visual optical cameras looking at roadside kilometer markers", "Satellite GPS location with zero trackside infrastructure"], "Trackside RFID tags provide absolute reference positioning, while train KAVACH computers dead-reckon speed and send radio telemetry to station towers."),
    ("What safety function does KAVACH execute during an emergency 'Head-On' collision trajectory between two trains?", "KAVACH automatically transmits emergency radio stop packets, initiating immediate emergency braking on BOTH locomotives simultaneously", ["It accelerates both trains to pass each other on single track", "It sounds the locomotive horn continuously without applying brakes", "It decouples passenger coaches from the locomotive engine"], "KAVACH operates peer-to-peer over direct wireless radio, applying emergency pneumatic brakes on both approaching trains without human intervention."),
    ("In railway bridge engineering, what does 'Bridge Scour' evaluate during monsoon river floods?", "The removal of underwater sediment and soil from around bridge piers and abutments caused by high-velocity flowing river water", ["The rust corrosion of steel bridge girders due to ocean salt air", "The thermal expansion of metal rails across long bridge spans", "The painting of bridge steelwork with protective epoxy primers"], "Scour undermines deep bridge pier foundations, threatening sudden structural bridge collapse during peak flood discharges."),
    ("In heavy haul rail freight, what is 'Distributed Power Wireless System' (DPWCS) in long-haul train operations (e.g., Super Vasuki)?", "Operating multiple locomotives distributed along a 3-km freight train controlled wirelessly by the lead driver via radio telemetry", ["Using two different railway tracks running parallel to each other", "Connecting locomotives together with long electrical extension cables", "Having a separate driver in each locomotive shouting over mobile phones"], "DPWCS balances tractive effort and braking throughout massive 295-wagon trains (Super Vasuki, 3.5 km long), eliminating coupler breakage."),
    ("Under Indian Railways structural reforms, what is the 'Indian Railway Management Service' (IRMS)?", "The unified civil service cadre merging 8 disparate legacy railway services to eliminate departmental silos and foster integrated governance", ["A private commercial consulting firm hired to manage train catering", "A specialized railway military infantry regiment for border defense", "A municipal committee managing passenger stations in New Delhi"], "Approved by the Union Cabinet, IRMS unifies technical and non-technical officers under a single merit-based cadre led by the CEO, Railway Board.")
]
